   same function, and only a hint: it lets a caller in another crate inline the
   function, and leaves whether to the compiler. Without it a `pub fn` is
   compiled once, kept as a symbol, and called from another crate however small
   it is (a `match` over a closed enum, a host calling it from one place). It is
   not `#[inline(always)]`: a body the compiler judges too large stays a call.
   Only a leaf carries it: `inline_hint` is false for a function that calls an
   algorithm or another function a document generates (the generator's
   `calls_no_generated_function`). Hinting a caller and its callees alike lets
   every crate that calls the caller copy both, and a large caller grows the
   code the hint was meant to shrink; whether the caller is inlined is the
   compiler's own judgement.
   Nor does a leaf another document of its set calls (`set_calls`): that caller
   is in the set's crate, which inlines a leaf it sees with no hint, and the
   hint there only moved the caller's register allocation. A leaf no document
   of the set calls, an entry point of the set, keeps it. #}
